FINDINGS:

1. [BLOCKER] src/lsp.rs:1803 — A module member replaced by a `set`/`constant` inside a nested block is not dropped from the published-function table. The guard is `frames.last().and_then(|frame| frame.module)`, and `Frame::scoped` sets `module: None` (src/lsp.rs:454); an `if`/`unless`/loop frame is pushed above the module frame (e.g. src/lsp.rs:1399), so `if yes then / set f to 2 / end` inside a module never reaches the removal at src/lsp.rs:1811-1813. The deferred pass at src/lsp.rs:2211-2216 then still maps `M.f()` to the original `to f`. At runtime a block `set` writes through to the innermost module frame (the report's own model, and `declare_member` at src/interpreter.rs:1544-1550 reads that module-scope value), so `M.f` is the replacement value while navigation answers the stale function declaration. Concrete fix: resolve the owning module by walking `runtime_scopes`/the frame stack rather than only `frames.last()`, and remove the function entry whenever the `set`/`constant` targets the module's runtime scope. Existing coverage only pins a top-level `set`/`constant` (tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:4077, :4103); no test covers the nested-block replacement. — VERIFIED: traced the frame path: `Frame::scoped` leaves `module: None` (src/lsp.rs:454), `If` pushes a scoped frame (src/lsp.rs:1399), and src/lsp.rs:1803 reads only `frames.last()`, so the removal at :1811 cannot fire for a branch `set`.

2. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1376 — The round-1 fix keys `declared_module_functions` by binding, but alias resolution still goes through a name-keyed table: `module_name_bindings.insert(name.clone(), module)` is overwritten on every same-named module close, and the deferred pass resolves an `Import` receiver via `module_aliases` → source name → `module_name_bindings` (src/lsp.rs:2206-2208). For a first module, a following `import M as A`, and a later second `module M`, `A.f()` is resolved against the *last* module's table, not the first module the import actually named. The new same-named test only exercises bare `M.f()` (tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:4039) and never an alias, so the stated invariant "two same-named declarations cannot overwrite each other" is not actually held for aliases. Concrete fix: record the module binding at the `import`/`as` site (alias → module binding) instead of looking the source name up in a global last-wins map. (Caveat: the interpreter refuses a second `module` of the same name at src/interpreter.rs:1567-1571, so this is navigation over source the runtime would reject; it is still an internal contradiction with the keying fix.)

